    "resolved_target_issue": null,
    "fix_pattern_detected": null,
    "is_reverted": false,
    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe root cause of the syntax error at line 209 is that the function firstPendingFIFO is incorrectly defined using an assignment (=) followed by a set comprehension in curly braces { }, which is not valid syntax for Alloy 6 function bodies. Alloy 6 requires function bodies to be defined as a single expression, and when using a set comprehension as the body, it must be enclosed in parentheses, not curly braces, and the entire expression must directly follow the '='. The current function definition:\nfun firstPendingFIFO[s: State]: lone Request =\n    { r: Request | s.reqStatus[r] = Pending and\n      no r2: Request | s.reqStatus[r2] = Pending and precedes[r2, r]\n    }\n...is invalid because it uses curly braces and is not syntactically recognized as a function body. This malformed function body causes the parser to misinterpret the subsequent 'pred precedes' declaration, flagging an error at line 209.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nRefactor firstPendingFIFO to use valid Alloy 6 function body syntax by enclosing the set comprehension in parentheses.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Locate the definition of firstPendingFIFO (currently at line 206).\n- Step 2: Replace the curly braces { ... } surrounding the set comprehension with parentheses ( ... ).\n- Step 3: Ensure the function body reads as:\n      fun firstPendingFIFO[s: State]: lone Request =\n          (r: Request | s.reqStatus[r] = Pending and\n           no r2: Request | s.reqStatus[r2] = Pending and precedes[r2, r])\n- Step 4: Remove any curly braces from the function body, and verify there is no trailing semicolon after the function, as Alloy 6 does not require one.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix correctly applies Alloy 6 function syntax by using parentheses for the set comprehension, ensuring the parser can distinguish where the function ends and the next top-level declaration begins; it is distinct from failed strategies that may have left curly braces or attempted to move the function without fixing its body format.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen writing function definitions in Alloy 6, always use parentheses for set or relational comprehensions in the function body; curly braces are reserved for predicate and fact bodies.\n```",
    "timestamp": "2026-06-12T13:00:13.322746"
  },
