    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error occurs at line 351, column 17, where the code attempts to use \"one as: set Administrator | #as = 2\" to express that exactly two administrators are involved in an Emergency activation. However, in Alloy 6, the \"one\" quantifier is only used for single-element binding, and its variable must be a single atom at a time, not a set. Attempting to bind \"as\" as a set of two Administrators with \"one as: set Administrator\" is not valid syntax. To express that exactly two distinct administrators are responsible, you should use \"some\" or \"one\" quantifiers over two distinct variables and explicitly state their distinctness and set cardinality.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nReplace the invalid \"one as: set Administrator | #as = 2\" quantification with a valid Alloy 6 expression that asserts exactly two distinct administrators are involved.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Navigate to the assertion at line 351 in the assertEmergencyActivationExactlyTwoAdmins block.\n- Step 2: Replace the line\n      one as: set Administrator | #as = 2\n  with\n      some disj a1, a2: Administrator | no Administrator - (a1 + a2)\n- Step 3: This asserts that there are exactly two distinct administrators (a1 and a2), and no other Administrator exists in the set.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix uses Alloy's \"some disj\" quantifier to bind two distinct administrators and ensures no others exist, which is valid Alloy 6 syntax and avoids the problematic \"one ... set\" quantification—thus complying with Alloy's type system and quantification rules.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen quantifying over sets with specific cardinality in Alloy, use multiple disjoint variable bindings rather than attempting to bind a set variable directly with \"one\" or \"some ... set\".\n```",
