    "evaluator_feedback": "## 1. Next Action Decision\n\n**Primary next action:**  \nrefine/narrow fix\n\n**Rationale:**  \nThe syntax repair was successful and caused no regressions, but persistent semantic failures remain (unsatisfiable EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario; counterexamples for several key assertions). No new regressions were introduced, but the current changes are not sufficient for convergence. The required fixes are more targeted semantic/model improvements, not a full revert nor a scope adjustment.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Concrete Model-Improvement Guidance\n\n**A. EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario Unsatisfiable (likely overconstraint/conflict):**\n- **Refactor Emergency request processing logic:**  \n  The current EmergencyProcessingPriority fact may be too restrictive, preventing any instance where a high-classification request can be processed ahead of a lower-classification one (i.e., bypassing FIFO). Specifically, the constraint:\n  ```all r: Request | (s.reqStatus[r] = Pending and s2.reqStatus[r] = Processed) => r in high```\n  may block all lower-classification requests from ever being processed if any high are present, and may not allow a \"bypass\" (processing high before FIFO low/med).\n- **Action:**  \n  - Replace the strict \"all\" condition with an \"exists\" or \"allows\" form, so that in Emergency, it is possible (but not required) to process a high-classification request ahead of a pending lower-classification request, even if the lower-classification one is earlier in FIFO order.\n  - Concretely, update the EmergencyProcessingPriority fact to allow for concurrent or prioritized processing of high-classification requests, without blocking eventual processing of others.\n- **Example code guidance:**\n  ```all s, s2: State |\n      s.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Emergency and Next[s, s2] =>\n          let pending = {r: Request | s.reqStatus[r] = Pending} |\n          let high = {r: Request | r in pending and requestClassification[r] = High} |\n          some r: high | s2.reqStatus[r] = Processed\n  ```\n  - Ensure that this does not overconstrain processing of medium/low requests once no high remain.\n\n---\n\n**B. assertRequestProcessorClearance (counterexample):**\n- **Likely cause:**  \n  The assertion attempts to model that the request processor's clearance meets or exceeds the request's classification, but the logic is convoluted and may not match intended clearance policy.\n- **Action:**  \n  - Replace the multi-case clearance check with a direct, total ordering comparison. Use the leqClearance function:\n  - For each processed request, require:  \n    `leqClearance[requestClassification[r], proc.clearance]`\n- **Example code guidance:**\n  ```all s: State, r: Request |\n      s.reqStatus[r] = Processed and some s.reqProcessor[r] =>\n          leqClearance[requestClassification[r], s.reqProcessor[r].clearance]\n  ```\n\n---\n\n**C. assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency (counterexample):**\n- **Likely cause:**  \n  The model may not always allow users to hold mutex roles during Emergency, possibly because role assignments are still constrained by mutex facts in Emergency.\n- **Action:**  \n  - Relax mutual exclusion constraints during Emergency mode. Ensure that the DelegationMutex fact only applies in Normal mode, and explicitly allow mutex roles in Emergency.  \n  - Review any facts or constraints that might globally prevent mutex role assignment.\n\n---\n\n**D. assertAdminsMustUpdateCredentialsOnce (counterexample):**\n- **Problem:**  \n  This assertion expects that after Emergency mode ends, all admins update credentials exactly once. The model's adminCredUpdated tracking and transition logic may not enforce this as a liveness property.\n- **Action:**  \n  - Add or refine a fact/predicate to require that after Emergency exit (in the first Normal state after Emergency), all admins must have adminCredUpdated[a]=True, and that this cannot occur in any other state.\n  - Ensure no transitions allow adminCredUpdated[a]=True except immediately after Emergency exit.\n\n---\n\n**E. assertRoleAssignmentClearance & assertDelegationClearance (counterexamples):**\n- **Likely cause:**  \n  Role assignment/delegation clearance is only asserted, not enforced by facts, so model instances may arise where assignment/delegation exceeds clearance.\n- **Action:**  \n  - Add facts enforcing these constraints:\n    - For all s: State, u: User, r: Role:  \n      `r in s.userRoles[u] => leqClearance[r.minClearance, u.clearance]`\n    - For all d: Delegation:  \n      `leqClearance[d.role.minClearance, d.to.clearance]`\n  - Remove these as \"pure\" assertions if you expect no exception; otherwise, document requirement for valid exceptions.\n\n---\n\n**F. assertAllRequestsEventuallyProcessed (counterexample):**\n- **Likely cause:**  \n  The model does not guarantee liveness: that after Emergency, all requests are eventually processed.\n- **Action:**  \n  - Enforce, via a liveness fact or stronger assertion, that each pending request must transition to Processed in some future Normal state.\n  - If model structure prevents liveness (e.g., no guarantee of transitions that process all requests), consider adding a progress fact:\n    ```all r: Request |\n        some s: State | s.mode = Normal and s.reqStatus[r] = Processed\n    ```\n    and ensure no facts prevent request processing transitions.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Assumptions to Review\n\nNo new flagged assumptions at this time. If model changes introduce new constraints or relaxations, flag those assumptions as `confirm` in the next round.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Requirement Update Recommendations\n\nNo requirements need update at this stage. All ambiguity and missing exceptions relate to modeling choices, not requirements text.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Q&A Reuse and Update\n\nNo Q&A reuse or new Q&A required at this stage.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Stronger Verification Checks (when hard metrics pass)\n\nOnce all counterexamples are resolved:\n- Add vacuity checks on Emergency privilege revocation.\n- Check negative cases (e.g., role mutex in Normal never violated).\n- Add edge-case scenarios for concurrent high-classification requests in Emergency.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Prioritized Handoff Items\n\n**Critical blockers to fix now:**\n1. Refine EmergencyProcessingPriority to allow EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario (A)\n2. Correct assertRequestProcessorClearance logic and enforce via fact (B)\n3. Relax mutex constraints in Emergency mode (C)\n4. Enforce admin credential update exactly once after Emergency (D)\n5. Enforce role assignment and delegation clearance in facts (E)\n6. Add/request liveness progress for all requests (F)\n\n**Important follow-up improvements:**\n- After semantic issues are addressed, expand property checks for vacuity and negative cases.\n- Review assumptions if new constraints are introduced.\n\n**Optional/future enhancements:**\n- Enhance Emergency scenario coverage (e.g., concurrent processing, edge cases).\n- Modularize processing logic for maintainability.\n\n---\n\n**Summary Table:**\n\n| Issue/Scenario                              | Action                                                                 | Priority     |\n|---------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------|\n| EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario unsat           | Refine EmergencyProcessingPriority to enable high-priority bypass       | Critical     |\n| assertRequestProcessorClearance fails       | Rewrite constraint using leqClearance; enforce as fact                 | Critical     |\n| assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency fails     | Relax mutex constraints during Emergency                               | Critical     |\n| assertAdminsMustUpdateCredentialsOnce fails | Enforce credential update timing; refine adminCredUpdated logic         | Critical     |\n| assertRoleAssignment/DelegationClearance    | Enforce clearance in facts, not just assertions                        | Critical     |\n| assertAllRequestsEventuallyProcessed fails  | Add strong liveness/progress fact for request processing               | Critical     |\n\nPlease address these critical model corrections before proceeding to further verification or requirements review.",
