    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: User clarified that Emergency processing must strictly order all high before all medium, and all medium before all low requests, and that processor clearance must be strictly higher than the submitter’s. The current model logic does not enforce these, so targeted refinement is required.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples\n- Instances: Suspicious (scenarios do not match clarified requirements)\n- Vacuity: None\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: User review revealed that the model does not enforce the strict hierarchical Emergency processing or the correct clearance constraint; further targeted fixes are required to align with clarified requirements.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n1. Emergency Request Processing Priority:\n   - **Location:** Fact or predicate governing request processing order during Emergency (e.g., EmergencyProcessingPriority).\n   - **Action:** Revise logic so that during Emergency, ALL high-classification requests must be processed before ANY medium, and ALL medium before ANY low. Overtaking is not merely permitted but strictly required.\n   - **Example (pseudo-Alloy):**\n     ```\n     fact EmergencyProcessingStrictPriority {\n       all s: State | s.mode = Emergency => \n         all r1, r2: Request | \n           (r1.class = High and r2.class = Medium or r2.class = Low and r1 in s.pending and r2 in s.pending) =>\n             r1.processedBefore[r2]\n         all r1, r2: Request | \n           (r1.class = Medium and r2.class = Low and r1 in s.pending and r2 in s.pending) =>\n             r1.processedBefore[r2]\n     }\n     ```\n   - Remove/replace any logic that allows \"permissive\" overtaking in favor of this strict ordering.\n\n2. Processor Clearance Constraint:\n   - **Location:** Predicate or fact where request processing is authorized (e.g., canProcess).\n   - **Action:** Ensure that, for a processor to process a request, their clearance is strictly greater than the submitter's clearance (not equal or lower).\n   - **Example (pseudo-Alloy):**\n     ```\n     pred canProcess[proc: User, req: Request] {\n       proc.clearance > req.submitter.clearance\n       // ... other existing conditions ...\n     }\n     ```\n   - Double-check that \"strictly higher\" is enforced everywhere, and that no residual permissions allow equal clearance.\n\n3. Regression Safeguards:\n   - Ensure that these changes do not weaken or alter Normal mode FIFO or mutex behavior.\n   - Retest all related assertions (Normal mode processing, mutex, Emergency transitions).\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - requirements are clear\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Refactor Emergency processing logic to strictly enforce: all high-classification requests processed before all medium, all medium before all low, with no exceptions.\n- Enforce that a processor’s clearance must be strictly higher than the submitter’s for request processing in all relevant predicates.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements):\n- Retest all assertions and scenarios to ensure no regressions, especially around role mutex and Normal mode FIFO.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements):\n- Add explicit negative and edge-case scenarios for Emergency request ordering and clearance logic to confirm no unintended behaviors.\n```",
